# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
DEFS = {
"A1": "Mechanistic Self-Direction is the near cause of a free choice, the working parts that let an actor choose, create, and explore at the moment it acts. It covers what the senses take in, the state of the body, the wiring that carries it, and the loops that make going one's own way the option taken rather than falling in with the rest. To ask after mechanism is to ask what goes on inside the actor and just around it right now, never why the trait is there at all. A signal of curiosity, a guess at a reward, a hold placed on a cued response, and the power to keep a goal of one's own against a pull the other way are each answers of this kind, and each can be measured and moved.",
"A2": "Mechanistic Stimulation is the here-and-now apparatus that propels the search for novelty, thrill, and challenge. How high the bar for arousal sits, how sharply a surprise is met, and how fast a repeated event stops being felt are its parts. Together they tell how a nervous system turns the strange into approach.",
"A3": "Mechanistic Hedonism is the working apparatus of pleasure and of the senses as it runs in real time. It asks what a receptor takes up. It asks how strong a thing feels. Those answers tell how the pleasure is made, how long it holds, and how fast it fades.",
"A4": "Mechanistic Achievement is the live system that shows skill against a bar. It is made of paced effort, caught slips, taken cues, kept aims, and word on how the try went.",
"A5": "Mechanistic Power is the near apparatus of status seeking and of control over persons and resources, comprising posture, voice, hormonal swing, the attention paid to rank cues, and the reading of who won a contest, all of which tell how dominance and deference are made inside one encounter.",
"A6": "Mechanistic Security is the working threat-detection and steadying apparatus of an actor. It is made up of vigilance, startle, defensive reflex, and the appraisal that marks a setting as safe or unsafe and then holds conduct steady until that marking is revised.",
"A7": "Mechanistic Conformity is the restraint-apparatus inhibiting an impulse likely to upset onlookers or breach an expectation.",
"A8": "Mechanistic Tradition is the near machinery of custom. It comprises copying and the cues that set off a rite. It takes in as well the stores that hold a handed-down practice and retrieve it on the days a culture marks out for it.",
"A9": "Mechanistic Benevolence is the immediate apparatus of care aimed at close associates. It is made up of affiliative signalling, the reading of distress, grooming, and the appraisal that sends help toward a known rather than an unknown recipient.",
"A10": "Mechanistic Universalism is the working apparatus of concern reaching past the familiar circle, out to strangers, to other kinds, and to the habitat all of them share. It is made up of taking the view of another, of pushing out the edge of a category on purpose, and of holding a picture of some far-off party who will never be met. Mechanism asks only what makes wide concern run at the moment it runs.",
"B1": "Developmental Self-Direction is the ontogeny of autonomous choosing across a whole lifetime. Undirected infant activity becomes juvenile exploration, and then contested adolescent independence. At last there comes a settled adult capacity to originate a goal unprompted.",
"B2": "Developmental Stimulation is the ontogeny of novelty seeking, from infant orienting through juvenile play to the calibrated risk taking of maturity.",
"B3": "Developmental Hedonism is the ontogeny of the pleasure repertoire. An early preference for sweetness and warmth becomes the acquired tastes of one whole life.",
"B4": "Developmental Achievement is the ontogeny of shown skill. First come the mastery tries of a babe in arms. Then comes graded task load in the years of play. At last set bars, rivals, and one's own eye on the self make the grown pattern of striving toward a mark a watcher can score.",
"B5": "Developmental Power is the ontogeny of rank, precedence, and the juvenile climb toward standing.",
"B6": "Developmental Security is the ontogeny of safety, how early attachment sets a later tolerance for risk.",
"B7": "Developmental Conformity is the ontogeny of restraint, the growth of impulse control and the learning of which expectations a group enforces.",
"B8": "Developmental Tradition is the ontogeny of inherited custom in one individual, the course of taking up a practice by watching elders and rehearsing it until it is habitual.",
"B9": "Developmental Benevolence is the ontogeny of care. It runs from contagious infant distress through toddler helping to the reasoned loyalty of an adult.",
"B10": "Developmental Universalism is the ontogeny of wide concern. It is the widening of the circle a person will count as owed something, out from kin to group to stranger.",
"C1": "Functional Self-Direction is the survival value of independent choosing, the advantage of acting on self-gathered information when copying a neighbour would mislead, delay, or misfire.",
"C2": "Functional Stimulation is the use of novelty: news, range, luck, reach, spread, and a way out when the old way fails.",
"C3": "Functional Hedonism is the fitness role of pleasure: reinforcement, preference, appetite, consummation, and renewed pursuit.",
"C4": "Functional Achievement is the use of shown skill. A clear record of it earns mates, tools, aid, rank, trust, and a better share of the work.",
"C5": "Functional Power is the payoff of rank, the access priority that status confers over contested resources.",
"C6": "Functional Security is caution's survival value, plus stability under threat.",
"C7": "Functional Conformity is the payoff of restraint, the collective cost avoided when a member holds back.",
"C8": "Functional Tradition is inherited custom's survival value, the advantage of carrying a solution already field-tested by earlier generations instead of re-deriving it from nothing. A practice persisting without ever being understood can still be adaptive, because the population, and not the individual, paid for every error.",
"C9": "Functional Benevolence is the payoff of caring for close associates.",
"C10": "Functional Universalism is the use of care that reaches well past the near circle. It is the gain in keeping a shared home, a far-off ally, an unrelated stranger, and a claimant not yet born in good standing.",
"D1": "Phylogenetic Self-Direction is the lineage history of autonomous choosing. It is the comparative record of where independent action turns up across related species. It asks how often that was gained or lost, and what it was built upon.",
"D2": "Phylogenetic Stimulation is the lineage history of novelty seeking, traced by comparing how related species explore and by reconstructing their common ancestral form.",
"D3": "Phylogenetic Hedonism is the lineage history of pleasure. It is the deep conservation of reward machinery across distant animal groups, and the later divergence of what each separate group finds gratifying.",
"D4": "Phylogenetic Achievement is the lineage past of shown skill, its ties to similar traits in kindred taxa, its rise, and its re-use in later lines.",
"D5": "Phylogenetic Power is the past of rank: pecking orders, precedence, deference, threat, appeasement, and their reach across many clades.",
"D6": "Phylogenetic Security is the past of defence: old, deep, kept, shared, and built on by all later lines.",
"D7": "Phylogenetic Conformity is restraint's lineage history, the comparative record of impulse-inhibition in taxa that live in groups with enforced, policed expectations.",
"D8": "Phylogenetic Tradition is the past of handed-on custom, the record of which kindred taxa carry local practices, and of how deeply in time that shared capacity can be traced back.",
"D9": "Phylogenetic Benevolence is the lineage history of care for associates, the comparative record of helping, grooming, food sharing, and provisioning in related species, and the ancestral form it was built upon.",
"D10": "Phylogenetic Universalism is the lineage history of wide concern. It is the rarest of these, and the most recently derived of them.",
}
